// The two capital rail cannons: the Resolute's spinal rail (`aster_t3_frigate`, fired
// down out of the cloud layer) and the Zenith (`aster_t4_anti_ship`, a ground gun
// firing up at warships). Nothing else in the game is this big, and they must never be
// mistaken for the rail family (`aster_rail_cannon` and its `like`s).
//
// Each shot is three sounds:
// - The charge (`charge_time` before the shot): capacitor banks switched in one after
//   another, as the Paladin's charge builds but plainly electrical. Each contactor
//   clacks; the banks' buzz (a rough growl, never a clean ringing note) and a
//   transformer growl under it climb; the air around the rails
//   fizzes, and the crackle thickens into a roar of sparks and arcs, all of it at its
//   loudest the instant the shot goes, then let go fast under the fire sound. The game
//   pitches every shot by up to 5%, so the top is held a moment and the release
//   overlaps the fire. The weight is in the 150 Hz to 3 kHz band: a charge carried
//   under 150 Hz is lost on small speakers and under a battle.
// - The fire: the slug through the sound barrier many times over (one hard N-wave crack,
//   front and back shock), the rails dumping (a heavy electrical slam, low and short, no
//   buzz), then a thunder that rolls for seconds, swelling and sagging off the country.
// - The strike: a kinetic slam, the target coming apart, debris falling after.
//
// The spinal rail is heard from the sky: a sharper crack, and the thunder crossing
// overhead from side to side. The Zenith is heard from beside it: the ground heaving,
// more weight under 60 Hz, and the report going up and away and coming back down
// off the cloud. Both fire sounds keep their body at 40 to 400 Hz; nothing in them
// whistles, rings or buzzes (the buzz belongs to the charge).
//
// Tuning notes: every Roll has a pinned `seed`. A Roll's random swells can outpeak the
// crack, and the whole sound is scaled to its loudest sample, so an unlucky swell pushes
// everything else down; the seeds keep the swells where they were measured (and adding
// a layer no longer reshuffles the others). The fire sounds end with a second, gentle
// Drive over the low thunder: that is what puts them well over the Leviathan's broadside
// in loudness at the same 0.9 peak, without clipping.
(
    sounds: {
        // ---- the Resolute's spinal rail cannon ------------------------------------
        // A heavy sequence, not one smooth climb, and plainly electrical: the capacitor
        // banks slam in one after another, closer together and heavier each time. Each
        // contactor clacks and an arc cracks across the rails with thunder rolling after
        // it. Under the steps the banks' rough buzz and the transformer growl climb, and
        // the crackle and sizzle along the rails thicken until the sparks come as a roar.
        // It starts low and ends huge: the last second is an overload, the banks
        // screaming up in a rough growl, the air along the rails roaring, the last banks
        // slamming in on top of each other and a final lock-on slam just before the shot,
        // some 15 dB over the first bank. The weight sits in the 150 Hz to 3 kHz band,
        // where it is heard from the ground under the ship and over a battle.
        "spinal_rail_charge": (
            length: 4.3, peak: 0.95, room: 0.4,
            layers: [
                // The first bank slams in: a hard contactor clack and a deep thunk.
                Burst(low: 250, high: 2000, attack: 0.0005, decay: 0.012, gain: 2.2, seed: 380),
                Tone(from: 120, to: 50, glide: 0.1, attack: 0.001, decay: 0.2, gain: 0.6),
                // The transformer growl climbing with the charge.
                Fm(at: 0.05, from: 36, to: 84, glide: 2.95, ratio: 0.5, index: 2.6, fade: 8.0, attack: 2.95, decay: 0.12, gain: 0.55),
                // The banks' buzz: a rough growl swelling and climbing the whole way, held
                // rough (a long `fade`), over a first bank's buzz that comes in at once.
                // Nothing here is a note that rings: a harmonic tone struck and left to
                // decay, or two ears detuned, reads as a plucked string.
                Fm(at: 0.005, from: 72, to: 168, glide: 2.95, ratio: 0.5, index: 2.8, fade: 12.0, attack: 2.9, decay: 0.1, gain: 0.55),
                Fm(at: 0.005, from: 72, to: 120, glide: 2.95, ratio: 0.5, index: 2.2, fade: 12.0, attack: 0.1, decay: 2.2, gain: 0.45),
                // Each bank after the first: a sub thunk, sooner, heavier, a notch higher.
                Tone(at: 0.8, from: 70, to: 39, glide: 0.2, attack: 0.002, decay: 0.300, gain: 0.55),
                Tone(at: 1.4, from: 78, to: 43, glide: 0.2, attack: 0.002, decay: 0.285, gain: 0.63),
                Tone(at: 1.9, from: 86, to: 47, glide: 0.2, attack: 0.002, decay: 0.270, gain: 0.71),
                Tone(at: 2.3, from: 94, to: 51, glide: 0.2, attack: 0.002, decay: 0.255, gain: 0.79),
                Tone(at: 2.6, from: 102, to: 55, glide: 0.2, attack: 0.002, decay: 0.240, gain: 0.87),
                Tone(at: 2.8, from: 110, to: 59, glide: 0.2, attack: 0.002, decay: 0.225, gain: 0.95),
                Tone(at: 2.92, from: 118, to: 63, glide: 0.2, attack: 0.002, decay: 0.210, gain: 1.03),
                // The last instant: a swell under everything.
                Tone(at: 2.2, from: 44, to: 60, glide: 0.8, attack: 0.8, decay: 0.12, gain: 0.45),
                // The overload: over the last second the banks scream up, a rough growl
                // (never a clean whine) climbing more than an octave into the mids, with a
                // harsher one an octave over it for the last half second.
                Fm(at: 2.0, from: 130, to: 290, glide: 0.98, ratio: 0.5, index: 3.2, fade: 12.0, attack: 0.98, decay: 0.08, gain: 0.9),
                Fm(at: 2.5, from: 260, to: 520, glide: 0.48, ratio: 0.5, index: 2.6, fade: 12.0, attack: 0.48, decay: 0.06, gain: 0.4, pan: 0.1),
                // The lock-on: a last heavy slam as the gun takes the whole charge.
                Tone(at: 2.97, from: 150, to: 60, glide: 0.1, attack: 0.002, decay: 0.2, gain: 1.2),
                Drive(1.7),
                Roll(at: 0.05, from: 800, to: 2200, glide: 2.9, attack: 1.2, decay: 0.1, gain: 0.3, swell: 110, depth: 1.0, pan: 0.1, seed: 371),
                // Each bank switching in: the contactor's clack, the arc it throws across the
                // rails (a crack and a short sizzle), and the air rolling after it.
                Burst(at: 0.8, low: 280, high: 2200, attack: 0.0005, decay: 0.012, gain: 1.43, seed: 381),
                Burst(at: 0.82, low: 350, high: 2600, attack: 0.0004, decay: 0.02, gain: 0.88, pan: -0.3, seed: 341),
                Roll(at: 0.82, from: 2400, to: 500, glide: 0.10, attack: 0.001, decay: 0.080, gain: 0.3, swell: 90, depth: 1.0, seed: 431),
                Roll(at: 0.83, from: 700, to: 90, glide: 0.5, attack: 0.012, decay: 0.50, gain: 0.34, swell: 11, depth: 0.8, pan: -0.3, seed: 391),
                Burst(at: 1.4, low: 280, high: 2200, attack: 0.0005, decay: 0.012, gain: 1.59, seed: 382),
                Burst(at: 1.42, low: 350, high: 2600, attack: 0.0004, decay: 0.02, gain: 0.94, pan: 0.35, seed: 342),
                Roll(at: 1.42, from: 2400, to: 500, glide: 0.11, attack: 0.001, decay: 0.088, gain: 0.3, swell: 90, depth: 1.0, seed: 432),
                Roll(at: 1.43, from: 700, to: 90, glide: 0.5, attack: 0.012, decay: 0.54, gain: 0.39, swell: 11, depth: 0.8, pan: 0.35, seed: 392),
                Burst(at: 1.9, low: 280, high: 2200, attack: 0.0005, decay: 0.012, gain: 1.76, seed: 383),
